---

# 🔐 SSH LOCAL PORT FORWARDING — FULL CHEAT SHEET

---

# 🧭 STEP 1 — Login to remote server (check service)

```bash id="a1"
ssh user@DB_SERVER_IP
```

```bash id="a2"
ss -tulnp | grep 5432
```

✔ Confirms PostgreSQL is running on remote server

---

# 🧭 STEP 2 — Ensure DB is NOT exposed (best practice)

On DB server:

```text id="a3"
postgresql.conf:
listen_addresses = 'localhost'
```

✔ DB only accessible inside server

---

# 🧭 STEP 3 — Firewall setup (secure production)

```bash id="a4"
sudo ufw allow 22/tcp
sudo ufw default deny incoming
sudo ufw default allow outgoing
```

✔ Only SSH is open to outside world
✔ DB ports are blocked (5432 not exposed)

---

# 🧭 STEP 4 — BASIC LOCAL PORT FORWARDING (MAIN COMMAND)

```bash id="a5"
ssh -L 15432:localhost:5432 user@DB_SERVER_IP
```

---

## 🧠 Meaning:

| Part              | Meaning                   |
| ----------------- | ------------------------- |
| 15432             | Your local machine port   |
| localhost         | Remote server itself      |
| 5432              | PostgreSQL port on server |
| user@DB_SERVER_IP | SSH login                 |

---

## 🔄 Flow:

```text id="a6"
Your Laptop:15432
      ↓
SSH Tunnel (port 22)
      ↓
Server:localhost:5432
      ↓
PostgreSQL DB
```

---

# 🧭 STEP 5 — Connect to DB (HOT CONNECT)

```bash id="a7"
psql -h localhost -p 15432 -U postgres
```

✔ Treat remote DB like local DB

---

# 🧭 STEP 6 — Run tunnel in background

```bash id="a8"
ssh -f -N -L 15432:localhost:5432 user@DB_SERVER_IP
```

### Flags:

| Flag | Meaning                |
| ---- | ---------------------- |
| `-f` | run in background      |
| `-N` | no shell (tunnel only) |
| `-L` | local forwarding       |

---

# 🧭 STEP 7 — Use different local port (if conflict)

```bash id="a9"
ssh -L 9999:localhost:5432 user@DB_SERVER_IP
```

Connect:

```bash id="a10"
psql -h localhost -p 9999 -U postgres
```

---

# 🧭 STEP 8 — WEB APP EXAMPLE (nginx / Podman)

```bash id="a11"
ssh -L 9090:localhost:8090 user@SERVER_IP
```

Open:

```text id="a12"
http://localhost:9090
```

---

# 🧭 STEP 9 — VERIFY TUNNEL

```bash id="a13"
ss -tulnp | grep 15432
```

or

```bash id="a14"
lsof -i :15432
```

✔ Confirms local port is active

---

# 🧭 STEP 10 — STOP TUNNEL

```bash id="a15"
pkill -f "ssh -L"
```

or close terminal

---

# 🔐 SECURITY SUMMARY

✔ DB NOT exposed publicly
✔ Only SSH (22) is open
✔ All DB traffic is encrypted
✔ Access is temporary (session-based)

---

# ❌ COMMON MISTAKES

* ❌ Using `-l` instead of `-L`
* ❌ Opening 5432 to internet
* ❌ Thinking 15432 must be open on server
* ❌ DB not running on server

---

# 🏁 FINAL RULE

```text id="a16"
SSH -L = Secure tunnel from local machine → remote service → localhost access
```

